Patch 50 -- indirect-call target census: where the hot dispatch actually goes.

The island cannot yet take the four functions that carry the frame because their
closures dispatch through function pointers held in WineD3D objects, and a static
analysis cannot say what those pointers hold. Concluding "cannot be armed" from
not knowing was withdrawn; this measures it.

It lives in the GUEST DLL deliberately. The hot roots are not armed, so in the
island they never execute and there is nothing there to observe; in the x86 build
they run every frame, against the same objects and the same pointer values the
island would see.

Twelve ops-dispatch sites on the hot paths are wrapped so the pointer is recorded
and then called, argument list and return value untouched:

    #define MGS2_P50_CALL(site, fp) (mgs2_p50_icall((site), (const void *)(fp)), (fp))

Bounded, memory-only, published at present like every other census here; read by
harness/island_icall_census.py. Each distinct target is classified once, in the
guest -- Box86 bridge (a CC 'S' 'C' thunk), inside wined3d.dll, or another module
-- and counted thereafter, so the hot path is a short scan and an increment.

Measured on the owner's heavy save through a real reinforcement encounter:

  buffer_ops->buffer_prepare_location   21,433,464 calls   4821.9/frame
                                        ONE distinct target, inside wined3d.dll
  parent_ops->wined3d_object_destroyed         117 calls      0.0/frame
                                        the only cross-module target, into d3d8

  wined3d-self  100.0%      other-module  0.0%

Every hot indirect call resolves to a function the island already compiles for
ARM. The reverse bridge layer that was priced as the blocking cost is, on this
path, essentially absent. The attract demo gives the same shape independently.

Not instrumented: the 694 gl_info->gl_ops sites, expected to be Box86 bridges to
the already-native driver, but expected is not measured.

